    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Myositis ossificans",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "Radiographical images of myositis ossificans in a 20-year-old woman with severe right groin pain for 1&nbsp;month. Coronal T1-weighted MRI demonstrates a rounded mass anteromedial to the right proximal femur (arrow) (shown here). Corresponding coronal T2-weighted MRI with fat saturation shows a mass-like region of heterogeneous increased T2 signal intensity (arrow) with ill-defined surrounding soft tissue oedema (arrowheads) (Fig.&nbsp;&lt;&lt;32059&gt;&gt;). Anterior posterior radiograph of the right medial thigh 5&nbsp;weeks after onset of symptoms demonstrates a well-defined calcified lesion in the right medial thigh (arrows) (Fig.&nbsp;&lt;&lt;32061&gt;&gt;). Axial CT shows the characteristic peripheral calcification (arrows) and lucent centre consistent with myositis ossificans. Contrast this with a soft tissue sarcoma, which typically demonstrates an ill-defined periphery and calcified, ossified&nbsp;centre (Fig.&nbsp;&lt;&lt;32062&gt;&gt;).",
    "source": "Stevens, Kathryn J",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/51/183/32058.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/51/183/thumb_32058.jpg"
  },
